WEBVTT

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:12.500
Welcome to Talkspots. Today I'm joined by John Kerriakou. John, thank you for coming on.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:13.860
My pleasure. Thanks for having me.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:20.420
So tell us a little bit about your background and how you got into CIA and kind of, I guess, your

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:30.180
story to nutshell, if you wouldn't mind. Right. So I joined the CIA in 1990 after being recruited

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:35.780
by my grad school professor who was actually a CIA officer undercover as a professor,

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:42.580
and joined the CIA in January of 1990, spent the first seven and a half years of my career

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:47.380
as an analyst working on Iraq. And then the second seven and a half years of my career

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:55.780
as an operations officer doing counterterrorism operations. I did very well, ended up becoming

00:00:55.780 --> 00:01:01.700
the chief of counterterrorism operations in Pakistan after 9-11, immediately after 9-11.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:07.620
Then I went back to headquarters as the executive assistant to the CIA's deputy director

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:19.220
for operations. And then left in 2005, well, formally in April of 2005, went into the

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:24.580
private sector for a while. But then I blew the whistle on the CIA's torture program

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:31.860
in a nationally televised interview on ABC News. That was controversial. In the meantime,

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:36.340
then I went to the Senate Foreign Relations Committee as the senior investigator.

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:41.380
And then as soon as I left the Foreign Relations Committee, the Justice Department

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:46.260
arrested me and charged me with five felonies related to my whistleblowing.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:52.180
They ended up dropping four of the charges. And I went to prison for 23 months,

00:01:52.900 --> 00:02:00.580
completely unapologetically. And since then, I've been kind of known as the anti-torture

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:06.580
whistleblower. It's become like an identity for me. Odd as that might sound.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:13.500
The time is now to learn a new career at Moore Park College. Whether you want to earn a certificate

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:17.900
or a degree right out of high school or you're returning adults who want to learn more

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:25.100
and earn more, your new career starts now at moreparkcollege.edu slash ce. You belong here.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:32.060
That's moreparkcollege.edu slash ce. Get ready for a magical holiday adventure at

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:37.420
Wild Lights at the teaching zoo at Moore Park College. Join us in November and December as

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:43.260
the zoo transforms into a dazzling wonderland filled with stunning light displays, festive

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:49.660
cheer, and unforgettable family fun. Light up your holiday spirit. Enjoy seasonal treats, music,

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:55.820
Santa, and awesome holiday vibes. Let's kick off the holiday season together at Wild Lights,

00:02:55.820 --> 00:03:04.400
at the teaching zoo, and Moore Park College. It seems just from a late person's perspective,

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:09.680
I have no law enforcement or military background whatsoever, or CIA FBI. I have nothing like

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:16.080
that. Just from a late person's perspective, I feel like, I don't know, 15-ish years ago,

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:20.640
20 years ago, there was a lot of talk about torture. I feel like torture, and obviously

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:26.000
because of your work, like you being a whistleblower, I guess the two questions I have is, one,

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:33.040
does torture even work? And secondly, has the American government stopped doing

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:38.640
torture? Are they just better at concealing it now? Well, the quick answer is torture

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:47.280
most definitely, absolutely does not work. Now, I would get pushed back on that from

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:53.040
the torturers at the CIA because the truth is this. Yes, eventually, the person you're

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:59.040
torturing is going to tell you what you want to know, but he will say literally anything

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:03.840
to get you to stop torturing him. And so in the end, you have to take everything he says,

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:09.520
turn it over to a team of analysts. They have to sort through all the garbage and the lies and the

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:14.080
trash and the obfuscations and the little white lies which are even more dangerous,

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:21.440
and come up with what's actually true. Well, the FBI has been interrogating people

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:26.720
since the Nuremberg trials in 1945 and 1946. They do it in a completely different way.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:33.840
They establish a rapport with a subject. They treat him with respect. They engage him in

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:42.480
conversation. And invariably, the subject opens up. And so the CIA not only got nothing actionable

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:48.240
by torturing its prisoners, but then everything those prisoners said was forbidden from being

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:52.720
used against them in a court of law. And that's why we still have 34 people at Guantanamo

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:57.440
who've not been charged with a crime and will never face a court of law.

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:04.300
Like they're just going to be permanent, I guess, detainees. Is that what they're called?

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:10.460
Yeah. At least three of them and probably as many as six of them will be permanent detainees,

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:18.220
which is also a war crime to detain people like that forever with no criminal charges

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:23.980
ever having been filed against them. So you have to decide if you want to be known as

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:28.140
the good guys, as the country that respects human rights, like you tell everybody else in

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:33.260
the world to do, or if you want to do the same kind of things to the terrorists that the terrorists

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:39.920
do to you. Well, in your opinion, I mean, do you feel like that terrorism,

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:46.400
torture, do you feel like that's still going on today? Like has it taken on different forms

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:51.600
or something like that? Well, that's a great question actually. And honestly, the answer

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:57.520
depends on your definition of terrorism, or I'm sorry, of torture. So with passage of the

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:07.200
McCain-Feinstein amendment in 2015, which John McCain credited me for, thank God, it was generous of him,

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:15.600
with passage of that amendment, it outlawed torture. It outlawed exactly those techniques

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:22.480
that the CIA was using against its prisoners. And it said specifically that the only interrogation

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:29.840
techniques that an American government employee or a private citizen working on behalf of the

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:36.000
American government can employ in an interrogation session are those techniques outlined in the

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:42.320
Army Field Manual. So the Army Field Manual doesn't permit any of the things that the CIA

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:48.480
was doing to its prisoners. And if you violate that, then you're subject to the federal torture

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:54.160
statutes. I say all the time that, you know, we've got a law in this country called the Federal

00:06:54.160 --> 00:07:01.440
Torture Act of 1946, which specifically outlaws exactly what we did to al-Qaeda terrorists.

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:10.320
In 1946, we executed Japanese soldiers who had waterboarded American prisoners of war.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:14.000
We executed them. It was a death penalty offense to waterboard somebody.

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:21.280
And then in January of 1968, the Washington Post ran a front page photograph of an American

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:26.000
soldier waterboarding a North Vietnamese prisoner. On the day that that picture was published,

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:30.720
the Secretary of Defense, at the time it was Robert McNamara, ordered an investigation.

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:37.520
The soldier was arrested. He was charged with torture. He was convicted and sentenced

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:46.720
20 years at hard labor at Fort Leavenworth. But then, like magic, in 2002, all that torture is

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:53.440
perfectly legal. Well, the law never changed. The law was never amended. We were the ones who

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:59.760
changed. And we decided to ignore the Federal Torture Act of 1946 and the United Nations Convention

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:04.480
Against Torture, which we were not only signatories to, we were the authors of.

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:10.640
Was there a person who led the charge to basically implement torture?

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:16.400
Yes. At the end of the day, it was George Tenet, who was the CIA director at the time.

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:22.240
But George was convinced to implement this program by some real do-gooders

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:27.440
in the CIA's counterterrorism center. Specifically, they were Jose Rodriguez,

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:34.240
Cofer Black, James Pavitt, who was the deputy director for operations,

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:40.720
John McLaughlin, who was the deputy director of the CIA, John Brennan, who was the executive

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:47.440
director of the CIA. These are very bad people who, even to this day, well, Pavitt

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:51.520
has passed away. But to this day, all the rest of them believe they were doing the right thing.

