00:21:30.160 --> 00:21:36.240
Jews did it, to the American Jews, I mean, to space aliens and like seriously,

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:42.720
and nanothermite paint and controlled demolitions and all kinds of crazy nonsense.

00:21:43.440 --> 00:21:50.160
The truth is this was carried out by Al Qaeda and the US government was asleep at the switch.

00:21:53.660 --> 00:21:58.620
And I think you mentioned in that same interview, you said something about the fact

00:21:58.620 --> 00:22:03.420
that the United States government that they looked at the people ended up committing 9-11

00:22:04.060 --> 00:22:10.220
as I wouldn't want to say friends, but just people that they could use. Am I misremembering how you

00:22:10.220 --> 00:22:14.940
said that? Yeah, I mean, yeah, no, you're not misremembering it. They believed that these guys

00:22:14.940 --> 00:22:19.820
were recruitable. They didn't believe that they were going to carry out the gravest

00:22:19.820 --> 00:22:24.860
terrorist attack in American history. They thought, all right, these are true believers,

00:22:24.860 --> 00:22:31.740
but we may be able to work with these guys. And there was never any hope ever

00:22:32.540 --> 00:22:40.110
that we could work with these guys. Interesting. I mean, that kind of jumps to today as the concept of,

00:22:40.750 --> 00:22:44.190
I mean, is the war on terror, is that technically still going?

00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:49.870
Technically, yeah, it is. Yeah, there is for all intents and purposes, there is no such thing

00:22:49.870 --> 00:22:54.910
anymore as Al Qaeda. Not really. I mean, there are offshoots of it. There's Al Qaeda in

00:22:54.910 --> 00:23:06.750
the Islamic Maghreb, which is in the Sahel area of Africa. There's Al Qaeda in the Islamic Crescent,

00:23:06.750 --> 00:23:13.230
which is, in part it became ISIS, in part it became the Nusratar Front, and now in part it

00:23:13.230 --> 00:23:21.310
actually controls Syria, another one of our many screw-ups. But the Al Qaeda that we fought in

00:23:21.310 --> 00:23:27.840
Afghanistan and Pakistan, it just simply doesn't exist anymore. So what makes these groups today,

00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:33.200
I mean, are they really that much different than Al Qaeda of, you know, 20, whatever, 23 years ago?

00:23:35.120 --> 00:23:42.000
I would say they are more radical than Al Qaeda was. At least in Syria, you know,

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:48.400
ISIS went to war with Al Qaeda because Al Qaeda wasn't radical enough. That was their position.

00:23:49.280 --> 00:23:56.480
So it's something that we have to continue fighting, like never, never stop fighting.

00:23:56.480 --> 00:24:03.150
Otherwise, they'll be the death of us. Because, I mean, you look at a country like Syria and

00:24:04.430 --> 00:24:08.990
I don't even understand fully what happened. I mean, obviously, we supplied some weapons

00:24:08.990 --> 00:24:12.910
to the people that ended up overthrowing Assad. And, you know, you hear these

00:24:12.910 --> 00:24:16.270
are radicals and then, you know, you hear, well, the American government wasn't really

00:24:16.350 --> 00:24:21.630
behind these guys. There's other people. But, I mean, the idea of there being fluidity between

00:24:21.630 --> 00:24:25.070
the different groups you're involved with, I mean, that exists, right? Where, you know,

00:24:25.070 --> 00:24:28.510
maybe two years you're part of whatever ISIS K and then you're part of

00:24:29.550 --> 00:24:33.550
whatever other group, right? Like, is there kind of movement between these groups?

00:24:33.550 --> 00:24:39.550
There is some movement between them. You know, Syria is very, very complicated.

00:24:40.270 --> 00:24:48.830
And you've got different countries supporting different groups, as well as having

00:24:50.270 --> 00:24:56.030
the United States supporting different groups. The CIA supported what used to be Al Qaeda

00:24:57.310 --> 00:25:04.030
in Syria. And the Defense Department supported an independent rebel group.

00:25:04.750 --> 00:25:11.470
And oftentimes, the Pentagon's group was fighting the CIA's group.

00:25:12.590 --> 00:25:16.110
Like, what do you hope to accomplish with something like that? Like, what kind of

00:25:16.110 --> 00:25:22.030
stupid, terrible policy is that? Do you feel like, overall, with American foreign policy,

00:25:23.630 --> 00:25:26.590
is it just sometimes, is it just totally

00:25:27.310 --> 00:25:34.510
erratic and nonsensical? Like, I look at Syria, and again, I'm no expert, but

00:25:35.070 --> 00:25:38.830
how does this even serve our interests? Well, to have Assad gone.

00:25:38.830 --> 00:25:45.790
It doesn't. It doesn't. Listen, Assad was a bad guy, right? He was a genocidal maniac

00:25:45.790 --> 00:25:52.510
against his own people. But he was a secularist. There are working cathedrals,

00:25:53.230 --> 00:25:59.550
Christian cathedrals in Syria. There are working synagogues in Syria. Everybody was

00:25:59.550 --> 00:26:05.630
free to practice their own religion. There were women in the cabinet and women in the parliament.

00:26:05.630 --> 00:26:11.070
And now the place is controlled by Al Qaeda. You know, I always believe that the devil you

00:26:11.070 --> 00:26:15.870
know is better than the devil you don't. And this group now is the devil we don't.

00:26:16.830 --> 00:26:23.870
So I fear for what's happening in Syria, and I fear for what likely will happen to the Christian

00:26:23.870 --> 00:26:29.550
community in Syria. It's an extremely dangerous situation. You know, there's another thing,

00:26:29.550 --> 00:26:36.110
too. I think I should probably mention. There's a famous quote by Gamal Abdel Nasser,

00:26:36.110 --> 00:26:42.110
the former president of Egypt. I actually had it hanging over my desk at the CIA.

00:26:42.750 --> 00:26:48.670
He said, the thing about you Americans is that you never make clear cut stupid decisions.

00:26:48.670 --> 00:26:53.950
You only make complicated stupid decisions, which makes us wonder if there is something to them

00:26:53.950 --> 00:27:02.960
which we are missing. And that's absolutely true. I think that that's a very interesting quote. I

00:27:02.960 --> 00:27:08.880
mean, yeah, just as a person just observing the news, I don't really understand what our

00:27:08.880 --> 00:27:14.080
policies are. I don't understand our aims, especially in the Middle East. I completely

00:27:14.080 --> 00:27:19.680
understand the aims of the Russia-Ukraine conflict. America just wants to bleed out Russia.

00:27:20.640 --> 00:27:24.560
If you want to just boil it down to that, I can get that. Do they have to do it?

00:27:24.560 --> 00:27:30.400
Probably not. I mean, Russia's, they have a way smaller economy, way fewer people.

00:27:31.280 --> 00:27:35.760
But if you want to do that, okay, at least I can understand that. But in the Middle East,

00:27:36.720 --> 00:27:40.000
I don't really understand. What's the objective?

00:27:40.640 --> 00:27:50.480
Right, exactly. At one time, the objective was to ward off war as well and as long as possible.

00:27:51.040 --> 00:27:59.920
And I think that's changed now. I think that the goal now is to support Israel, period,

00:27:59.920 --> 00:28:04.560
regardless of who happens to be the prime minister and what those policies of that

00:28:04.560 --> 00:28:08.800
prime minister happen to be. And no matter how right-wing the prime minister becomes,

00:28:09.760 --> 00:28:17.920
no matter how right-wing the government becomes, and we see that now with people like

00:28:19.200 --> 00:28:26.400
Inamar Ben-Kavir and Bezala Smotrich, both members of the cabinet, who have been convicted of

00:28:26.960 --> 00:28:30.000
hate crimes, by the way, felony convictions for hate crimes against Arabs.

00:28:30.800 --> 00:28:35.760
And then what you do to make Israel safer, in addition to just feeding them with

00:28:37.760 --> 00:28:42.560
all the weapons and weapons systems that they could possibly use, is you get the Saudis,

00:28:42.560 --> 00:28:49.360
the Bahrainis, the Emiratis, the Kuwaitis, the Sudanese, the Moroccans, the Tunisians

00:28:49.360 --> 00:28:54.080
to open diplomatic relations with Israel. And there's really nothing in it for them.

00:28:55.040 --> 00:28:56.560
It only benefits Israel.

00:29:00.050 --> 00:29:03.970
Yeah. I mean, it certainly makes you wonder with American foreign policy that,

00:29:05.410 --> 00:29:10.930
I mean, how are the actual average Americans, how are they benefiting off of this?

00:29:10.930 --> 00:29:11.090
Yeah. Exactly.

00:29:11.090 --> 00:29:14.850
I mean, you look at the Arab Spring all these years late, or even if you go back further,

00:29:14.850 --> 00:29:21.890
from this century to today right now, so 2000s to right now, we've spent how much money?

00:29:21.890 --> 00:29:24.130
Trillions, by at least two trillion, if not more.

00:29:24.130 --> 00:29:24.850
Oh, yeah.

00:29:24.850 --> 00:29:29.570
And I would say the Middle East, well, I mean, obviously you were an expert in the field.

00:29:29.570 --> 00:29:34.050
I mean, how much would you say the Middle East is a better place today or a worse place

00:29:34.050 --> 00:29:36.370
and more dangerous to a world like peace?

00:29:36.370 --> 00:29:41.730
Oh, I think it's far more dangerous today than it was even five years ago, far more dangerous.

00:29:42.690 --> 00:29:47.010
You know, Lebanon is a failed state. Yemen is a failed state.

00:29:47.010 --> 00:29:52.610
We're practically in a state of war with Iran. Israel, I'm sorry, Syria has collapsed.

00:29:53.250 --> 00:30:00.290
And it's just in a state of chaos. The Israelis are occupying Syrian territory.

00:30:00.290 --> 00:30:04.450
The Americans are occupying Syrian territory. The Russians are bugging out.

00:30:04.450 --> 00:30:06.930
They're abandoning their bases. It's just chaos.

00:30:09.680 --> 00:30:12.480
I mean, who's to blame for what's going on today?

00:30:12.480 --> 00:30:14.160
Oh, there's like in America.

00:30:14.160 --> 00:30:15.920
There's a lot of blame to go around.

00:30:17.920 --> 00:30:24.320
Donald Trump's much-vaunted Abraham Accords, I think, were a disaster for peace.

00:30:24.320 --> 00:30:27.760
When Donald Trump said that he could solve the Middle East process

00:30:27.760 --> 00:30:33.440
or Middle East problem in a day, that meant by just screwing the Palestinians.

00:30:35.840 --> 00:30:40.080
I know, you know, you just, you give the Israelis everything they want

00:30:40.080 --> 00:30:45.200
and in exchange, they open relations with Saudi Arabia, the Emirates and Bahrain,

00:30:45.200 --> 00:30:48.800
and a couple of others. What would you do for the Palestinians?

00:30:48.800 --> 00:30:53.440
You've just screwed them. And now the Israelis are starting to annex more land.

00:30:55.870 --> 00:30:58.350
When you first started on the CIA back in the 90s,

00:30:59.150 --> 00:31:04.030
was that really a belief amongst analysts and people in the agency

00:31:04.030 --> 00:31:07.310
that there would be a Palestinian state?

00:31:07.310 --> 00:31:09.870
Like, was that something that people thought was actually going to happen?

00:31:09.870 --> 00:31:11.630
Oh my gosh, yes. And I'll tell you a story.

00:31:14.110 --> 00:31:19.230
My first boss at the CIA became the senior advisor to the president for the Middle East.

00:31:20.190 --> 00:31:28.670
And in January of 2001, just the final, final days of Bill Clinton's presidency,

00:31:29.550 --> 00:31:35.870
my friend was with Vice President Gore at Camp David with the Prime Minister of Israel

00:31:35.870 --> 00:31:39.470
and with Yasser Arafat, the head of the Palestine Liberation Organization.

00:31:40.350 --> 00:31:45.310
They had agreed on almost everything related to a peace deal.

00:31:45.310 --> 00:31:49.630
And they had an enormous map of Jerusalem on a conference room table.

00:31:49.630 --> 00:31:53.550
And they were literally dividing Jerusalem block by block.

00:31:54.590 --> 00:31:58.110
And when they finished, Gore said, my God, we have peace.

00:31:59.550 --> 00:32:04.350
And Arafat said, I can never sell this to the Palestinian people.

00:32:04.350 --> 00:32:07.310
And he walked out the door and Gore ran out after him.

00:32:07.310 --> 00:32:11.550
And he said, wait a minute, you can't just walk out after all these months,

00:32:11.550 --> 00:32:13.310
these years of negotiation.

00:32:13.310 --> 00:32:17.310
If there's something you don't like, tell us and we'll negotiate it.

00:32:17.870 --> 00:32:21.710
And he said, I can never sell a peace deal to the Palestinian people.

00:32:22.510 --> 00:32:24.350
And that was the death of the peace process.

00:32:25.150 --> 00:32:32.030
Well, now in retrospect, anything would have been better than nothing.

00:32:32.750 --> 00:32:35.310
And what the Palestinians have today is nothing.

00:32:37.310 --> 00:32:39.310
Why couldn't he sell it to the Palestinian people?

00:32:39.310 --> 00:32:45.950
Because at the time, the Palestinian mantra was to push the Jews into the sea.

00:32:47.470 --> 00:32:51.950
That was the goal, that all of Israel must be Palestine.

00:32:52.590 --> 00:32:55.710
And all Palestinian refugees had to have the right of return.

00:32:56.670 --> 00:32:58.510
The Jews would be pushed into the sea.

00:32:58.510 --> 00:33:00.350
And that's just not realistic anymore.

00:33:03.310 --> 00:33:06.350
What's the stance today?

00:33:06.910 --> 00:33:08.990
Do the Palestinians still say that same thing?

00:33:08.990 --> 00:33:10.990
Or is it just like, we'll literally take anything?

00:33:11.550 --> 00:33:15.070
Yeah, now there's no real consensus.

00:33:15.070 --> 00:33:17.950
So you've probably heard about the so-called two-state solution.

00:33:19.070 --> 00:33:24.350
The official US government policy is that we support a two-state solution.

00:33:24.350 --> 00:33:28.350
An independent Israel and an independent Palestine.

00:33:29.310 --> 00:33:31.790
That will never happen, period.

00:33:32.430 --> 00:33:33.630
It will never happen.

00:33:34.510 --> 00:33:41.550
Now, everybody says, well, the best anybody can hope for is a one-state solution

00:33:41.550 --> 00:33:47.310
where there's equality for Palestinians and they just live in Israel.

00:33:47.310 --> 00:33:50.510
That also will never happen, ever.

00:33:51.550 --> 00:33:54.750
I have a friend who spent 35 years in the Middle East

00:33:54.750 --> 00:33:57.710
as an American diplomat with the State Department.

00:33:58.430 --> 00:34:03.070
And he opines, and I know he does it kind of tongue in cheek,

00:34:03.070 --> 00:34:04.510
but I think he's onto something.

00:34:05.150 --> 00:34:06.750
You've got to have a three-state solution.

00:34:08.350 --> 00:34:13.470
The PLO, or what is now called the PA, the Palestinian Authority,

00:34:13.470 --> 00:34:14.670
controls the West Bank.

00:34:15.230 --> 00:34:18.510
Hamas controls Gaza for all intents and purposes.

00:34:19.070 --> 00:34:21.310
And never the two shall meet.

00:34:22.190 --> 00:34:24.910
And so if there is going to be a Palestinian state,

00:34:24.910 --> 00:34:27.950
it really has to be two Palestinian states.

00:34:27.950 --> 00:34:29.710
That's the best they're going to get.

00:34:29.710 --> 00:34:34.670
And do you feel like that's actually even possible?

00:34:34.670 --> 00:34:36.430
No, it's not possible.

00:34:36.430 --> 00:34:39.870
I think the only thing that's possible is the status quo,

00:34:40.670 --> 00:34:44.030
where Israel runs the equivalent of an apartheid state.

00:34:44.590 --> 00:34:49.790
Palestinians will always be at best second-class citizens,

00:34:49.790 --> 00:34:51.710
and they will never have a homeland.

00:34:51.710 --> 00:34:58.270
And the Israelis will continue to occupy

00:34:59.150 --> 00:35:02.110
and to annex Palestinian land.

00:35:04.750 --> 00:35:09.070
I mean, just from an American foreign policy perspective,

00:35:09.070 --> 00:35:13.150
I mean, would we be better off served if there were two states?

00:35:13.150 --> 00:35:14.110
Absolutely.

00:35:14.110 --> 00:35:16.350
To me, I don't really fully understand, again,

00:35:16.350 --> 00:35:18.030
when it comes to our policy in the Middle East,

00:35:18.030 --> 00:35:22.910
that I feel like we created enemies in places

00:35:22.910 --> 00:35:24.350
that we didn't really need to make it.

00:35:24.350 --> 00:35:25.630
Oh yeah, you're exactly right.

00:35:26.350 --> 00:35:27.230
You're exactly right.

00:35:27.310 --> 00:35:32.190
Richard Nixon made a decision in 1969

00:35:32.190 --> 00:35:35.310
to back Israel 100% with no questions asked.

00:35:35.310 --> 00:35:37.950
That changed American foreign policy permanently,

00:35:38.830 --> 00:35:42.270
and we'll never go back.

00:35:43.630 --> 00:35:46.430
We'll never be seen by Arabs as an honest broker

00:35:47.710 --> 00:35:49.230
in Israel and Palestine.

00:35:49.230 --> 00:35:52.990
Never. We picked a side, and we've stuck with it.

00:35:54.590 --> 00:35:56.430
Because I think from the media's perspective,

00:35:56.990 --> 00:35:58.670
maybe with the average person, I think,

00:35:59.470 --> 00:36:00.270
they'll say something like,

00:36:00.270 --> 00:36:02.830
well, radical Muslims don't like America because

00:36:03.710 --> 00:36:06.910
of our freedom, or maybe they go a little deeper and they say,

00:36:06.910 --> 00:36:08.750
well, there's too many Christians in this country,

00:36:08.750 --> 00:36:10.270
and they hate us because of those things.

00:36:10.990 --> 00:36:14.510
In reality, I mean, let's just take World War II,

00:36:15.070 --> 00:36:17.790
America was way, the percentage of people

00:36:17.790 --> 00:36:19.870
that said they were Christians was way higher than today.

00:36:19.870 --> 00:36:20.430
Yes.

00:36:20.430 --> 00:36:21.630
I feel like in a lot of ways you can say

00:36:21.630 --> 00:36:23.950
we actually had more individual freedoms than now.

00:36:23.950 --> 00:36:25.710
I mean, every phone call you ever make

00:36:25.710 --> 00:36:28.510
can be listened to or email or text or whatever.

00:36:29.470 --> 00:36:32.110
And these Middle Eastern countries didn't hate us then,

00:36:32.110 --> 00:36:34.270
at least not to this same vitriolic level.

00:36:34.270 --> 00:36:36.590
Yes. Yeah, I think that's right.

00:36:38.030 --> 00:36:43.310
I think we're becoming far more divided in this country.

00:36:43.310 --> 00:36:45.950
And it's funny to me too, because my own view

00:36:45.950 --> 00:36:47.950
is that there's really not much difference

00:36:47.950 --> 00:36:49.950
between the Democrats and the Republicans.

00:36:49.950 --> 00:36:52.110
I mean, you can sort of tinker around the edges,

00:36:52.110 --> 00:36:54.110
but they're both capitalist parties.

00:36:54.190 --> 00:36:56.830
They both agree on 90% of everything.

00:36:56.830 --> 00:37:00.670
And the Democrats have moved decisively to the right

00:37:01.230 --> 00:37:02.670
since 1972.

00:37:03.870 --> 00:37:05.310
I don't know why we're so divided.

00:37:05.310 --> 00:37:05.950
We shouldn't be.

00:37:09.070 --> 00:37:12.510
Yeah, that's very interesting, because even I've listened

00:37:12.510 --> 00:37:13.710
to some things about that too,

00:37:13.710 --> 00:37:19.870
that especially with Clinton just being a pro-free trader.

00:37:20.510 --> 00:37:22.350
And I wouldn't say anti-union,

00:37:22.430 --> 00:37:24.670
but you can definitely see in the parties now

00:37:24.670 --> 00:37:28.430
the idea of the worker, the working class, so to speak.

00:37:28.990 --> 00:37:31.310
I mean, both parties in a lot of ways are very much controlled

00:37:31.310 --> 00:37:32.670
by people that have a lot of money.

00:37:32.670 --> 00:37:33.150
Right, right.

00:37:33.150 --> 00:37:35.710
It's not, who's the union party today?

00:37:36.430 --> 00:37:38.030
Yeah, exactly.

00:37:38.030 --> 00:37:40.590
And this is something that unions are struggling with,

00:37:40.590 --> 00:37:44.190
because historically, unions have supported Democrats

00:37:44.190 --> 00:37:46.990
and most working people identified as Democrats.

00:37:46.990 --> 00:37:50.110
That started to change with the election of 1980

00:37:50.110 --> 00:37:51.870
and Ronald Reagan's victory.

00:37:51.870 --> 00:37:55.070
And it changed decisively with Donald Trump,

00:37:55.070 --> 00:38:00.990
where almost two-thirds of union households voted for Donald Trump.

00:38:00.990 --> 00:38:03.950
The Democrats have to really take a good hard look at themselves

00:38:03.950 --> 00:38:06.510
to figure out why they have lost working people.

00:38:09.150 --> 00:38:12.430
You mentioned, or again, it's just another interview

00:38:12.430 --> 00:38:13.150
that you did.

00:38:13.150 --> 00:38:16.190
I heard you talked about your work with John Kerry.

00:38:16.190 --> 00:38:16.510
Right.

00:38:17.630 --> 00:38:19.790
Again, just as an observer,

00:38:20.590 --> 00:38:24.030
obviously, I remember when John Kerry ran for president in 2004

00:38:24.030 --> 00:38:28.670
and after he lost, I just felt like he was just a senator again.

00:38:28.670 --> 00:38:29.870
He was just doing his own thing.

00:38:29.870 --> 00:38:31.310
Obviously, he was an influential person,

00:38:31.310 --> 00:38:32.350
because all senators are.

00:38:32.350 --> 00:38:36.750
But what happened that he seemed like,

00:38:36.750 --> 00:38:38.670
well, obviously, during Obama's second term

00:38:38.670 --> 00:38:39.790
becomes the Secretary of State.

00:38:39.790 --> 00:38:41.870
Like, what elevated him to that level?

00:38:41.870 --> 00:38:43.470
And it seems like since that time,

00:38:43.470 --> 00:38:45.390
he incorrect me if I'm wrong.

00:38:45.390 --> 00:38:49.630
It seems like he's been a person that almost got a boost

00:38:49.630 --> 00:38:52.830
or like a promotion in terms of importance

00:38:52.830 --> 00:38:54.190
to American foreign policy.

00:38:54.190 --> 00:38:56.030
Like, am I wrong about that?

00:38:56.030 --> 00:38:58.030
Or I feel like he, I mean, he lost,

00:38:58.030 --> 00:39:00.590
but after that, I feel like he's wielded a lot of power.

00:39:00.590 --> 00:39:01.630
Oh, yeah, yeah.

00:39:01.630 --> 00:39:07.230
Yeah, you know, Kerry ran for Congress first in 1972 and lost.

00:39:07.230 --> 00:39:09.950
And then he got elected Lieutenant Governor of Pennsylvania,

00:39:09.950 --> 00:39:11.710
I'm sorry, of Massachusetts.

00:39:11.710 --> 00:39:15.150
And then he ran for Congress, won a couple of terms there.

00:39:15.150 --> 00:39:17.710
And then when Paul Songus left the Senate,

00:39:17.710 --> 00:39:19.310
Kerry got himself elected to the Senate.

00:39:19.310 --> 00:39:21.470
He got onto very powerful committees,

00:39:22.270 --> 00:39:24.030
including the Finance Committee,

00:39:24.030 --> 00:39:29.870
which writes tax law and the Foreign Relations Committee.

00:39:31.470 --> 00:39:35.310
He was around long enough that he became the chairman

00:39:35.310 --> 00:39:37.310
of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee.

00:39:37.310 --> 00:39:41.550
And then ran for president, as you said, in 2004.

00:39:42.110 --> 00:39:48.030
He sort of promoted himself as sort of

00:39:49.950 --> 00:39:52.750
you know, that, I don't know, I'm trying to be nice.

00:39:54.350 --> 00:39:56.350
The senior statesman, right?

00:39:56.350 --> 00:39:58.030
The senior statesman in government.

00:39:58.910 --> 00:40:02.350
And when I was working for him, I mean, presidents,

00:40:02.350 --> 00:40:06.270
prime ministers would call him literally every single day

00:40:07.390 --> 00:40:08.750
just to consult.

00:40:09.870 --> 00:40:11.470
He believed he was going to be

00:40:11.470 --> 00:40:13.230
Barack Obama's first secretary of state.

00:40:13.230 --> 00:40:13.870
He wasn't.

00:40:13.870 --> 00:40:16.190
He was the second for political reasons.

00:40:17.150 --> 00:40:20.590
And then even under Biden, because they were old friends,

00:40:20.590 --> 00:40:21.950
he replaced Biden as the chairman

00:40:21.950 --> 00:40:23.390
of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee.

00:40:24.030 --> 00:40:27.150
He joined as the special envoy for the environment,

00:40:27.150 --> 00:40:28.030
I think is what it was.

00:40:29.070 --> 00:40:30.110
He's an old man now.

00:40:30.110 --> 00:40:31.630
He's nearing 80.

00:40:31.630 --> 00:40:35.230
But he still wants to remain part of the game.

00:40:35.230 --> 00:40:36.910
To tell you the truth, I'm not sure

00:40:37.790 --> 00:40:40.590
that John Kerry really contributes much anymore.

00:40:40.590 --> 00:40:43.150
I think it's time to go to a younger generation.

00:40:45.200 --> 00:40:46.960
Yeah, I was literally just looking him up right now.

00:40:46.960 --> 00:40:49.440
And yeah, I mean, he's 81 now.

00:40:49.440 --> 00:40:51.360
And I think the last position he was in

00:40:51.360 --> 00:40:54.720
was this special presidential envoy for climate.

00:40:54.720 --> 00:40:55.280
Yeah.

00:40:55.280 --> 00:40:56.000
Like, yeah.

00:40:56.000 --> 00:40:57.600
I guess that was interesting because he compared him

00:40:57.600 --> 00:41:00.000
to someone like Al Gore and Al Gore.

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:02.000
Obviously he did a lot of climate change things

00:41:02.000 --> 00:41:02.640
after the facts.

00:41:02.640 --> 00:41:04.960
And I'm sure people would take Al Gore's calls today.

00:41:04.960 --> 00:41:07.600
But it seems like Kerry really got elevated.

00:41:07.600 --> 00:41:08.000
Yeah, he did.

00:41:08.000 --> 00:41:08.720
Like, oh, yeah.

00:41:09.440 --> 00:41:10.560
Yeah, it's funny, right?

00:41:10.560 --> 00:41:12.160
But Al Gore also, you know,

00:41:12.160 --> 00:41:13.520
Al Gore turned to Hollywood.

00:41:13.520 --> 00:41:14.800
He moved to Santa Barbara.

00:41:14.800 --> 00:41:17.920
He won an Oscar for a documentary that he made.

00:41:19.200 --> 00:41:22.160
He really went on to do something different.

00:41:22.160 --> 00:41:24.640
And Kerry stayed right here in Washington.

00:41:28.340 --> 00:41:29.780
You said because of political reasons

00:41:29.780 --> 00:41:33.380
he was the secretary of state for Obama,

00:41:33.380 --> 00:41:34.500
where were those reasons?

00:41:34.500 --> 00:41:36.820
I mean, was it just that Hillary was?

00:41:36.820 --> 00:41:37.940
Yeah, Hillary, Hillary, Hillary.

00:41:37.940 --> 00:41:38.740
And she always held more,

00:41:39.300 --> 00:41:40.660
I mean, she always held more clout

00:41:40.660 --> 00:41:41.620
than even John Kerry.

00:41:41.620 --> 00:41:43.220
I mean, obviously because of her husband

00:41:43.220 --> 00:41:44.980
and the Clintons are so powerful.

00:41:45.620 --> 00:41:47.940
Yeah, yeah, it was all about Hillary.

00:41:47.940 --> 00:41:51.460
See, the Hillary Obama race was very, very close.

00:41:51.460 --> 00:41:55.460
Obama ended up with like 55% to Hillary's 45%.

00:41:55.460 --> 00:41:57.620
So he had to make the Hillary people happy.

00:41:58.260 --> 00:42:03.620
And doing that meant making Hillary the secretary of state.

00:42:04.420 --> 00:42:07.220
He had also promised sex state to Kerry

00:42:07.220 --> 00:42:08.980
and he had promised it to Bill Richardson

00:42:10.580 --> 00:42:12.420
and walked away from both of them.

00:42:14.460 --> 00:42:15.660
What did Bill Richardson,

00:42:15.660 --> 00:42:17.420
what did he do in the Obama administration?

00:42:17.420 --> 00:42:19.260
Was he ever something?

00:42:19.260 --> 00:42:20.300
Was he like the head of education?

00:42:20.300 --> 00:42:23.420
No, he was named secretary of commerce

00:42:23.420 --> 00:42:28.060
and he quit before it ever got any wheels.

00:42:29.180 --> 00:42:32.700
Oh, so he actually never served in the Obama administration?

00:42:32.700 --> 00:42:33.820
No, no.

00:42:36.220 --> 00:42:36.780
Interesting.

00:42:38.620 --> 00:42:39.740
Well, John, well, definitely.

00:42:39.740 --> 00:42:41.260
I want to thank you so much for coming on.

00:42:41.260 --> 00:42:43.740
Where can people watch your stuff or listen to your stuff?

00:42:43.740 --> 00:42:44.700
Oh, thanks for asking.

00:42:44.780 --> 00:42:47.660
I put everything I do on Substack.

00:42:47.660 --> 00:42:50.860
So if you go to Substack and just search John Kiriakou, there I am.

00:42:52.380 --> 00:42:54.540
And do you still like, you mentioned like,

00:42:54.540 --> 00:42:57.500
do you have like a YouTube channel

00:42:57.500 --> 00:42:59.900
or podcast that you like put things on regularly?

00:42:59.900 --> 00:43:01.820
Yeah, just starting to get it up.

00:43:01.820 --> 00:43:05.820
And because it's sort of scattered all over the internet right now,

00:43:05.820 --> 00:43:08.380
you know, I have columns at two different magazines

00:43:08.380 --> 00:43:10.780
and I just put everything on Substack.

00:43:11.340 --> 00:43:12.460
All right, John, well, thank you so much.

00:43:12.460 --> 00:43:14.060
I appreciate your time today and I'll talk to you.

00:43:14.060 --> 00:43:15.340
Thanks. Good to talk to you.

